# elm means eml

The product, operator, files, and CLI are **EML** / `eml`. The paper calls the operator `eml` (Exp-Minus-Log).

If the user says **elm**, **ELM**, **.elm**, **.telm**, or `elm …` in chat, interpret that as **eml**, **EML**, **.eml**, **.teml**, or `eml …` unless they clearly mean something else.

## Format names (do not confuse)

| Name | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **mxeml** / `.mxeml` | Mathematical expression language (former math `.teml`) |
| **teml** / `.teml` | Nested tree text only (`1` \| `eml(S, S)`) |
| **eml** / `.eml` | Stack IR text (`ONE` / `EML`) |
| **beml** / `.beml` | Binary stack IR |

If the user says “math `.teml`”, “source expression `.teml`”, or refers to the old math language as `.teml`, they mean **`.mxeml`** / `mxeml`. Nested tree files are `.teml`.

## Keep these as elm

- The local clone folder name (this checkout may stay `elm`)
- Anything outside this repository
- Historical plan files under `.cursor/plans/` (audit trail; do not rewrite them for this rename)

## Examples

- “run elm tokenize on that .telm” → `eml tokenize` on a `.teml` file (nested), unless they clearly mean the math language → then `.mxeml`
- “math source” / “operators and sin/cos” → `.mxeml`
- “IR ELM” / `elm(x, y)` → IR EML / `eml(x, y)`
- “add a test in elm_tests” → `tests/eml_tests.adb`
